quote:
Decision authority. Deployment decisions rest with the CTO, supported by the two technical leads (System Architect and Lead Software/AI Engineer). For client-facing systems, deployment additionally requires explicit client acceptance following user acceptance testing (UAT) — no AI feature moves to production on MGOIT's decision alone.
Evidence and criteria required before deployment:
• Functional and performance validation in a staging environment mirroring production, using anonymised or synthetic data;
• Edge-case and adversarial testing of AI outputs, including checks for bias, incorrect or fabricated outputs, and failure modes on malformed inputs;
• Security review consistent with our ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls: access control, encryption in transit and at rest, secrets management, and data-flow review (what data reaches which model provider, under what terms);
• Privacy and compliance check: GDPR lawful basis, data minimisation, and anonymisation applied to any personal data in AI pipelines;
• Confirmation that graduated human-review mechanisms (per our risk criteria) are implemented and functioning, not merely designed.
Interpreting evaluation results. We treat evaluation metrics as context-bound: results obtained on test data are considered indicative, not guarantees, particularly for generative components where output quality varies with real-world input distribution. Known limitations (e.g. domains where the model underperforms, language coverage, edge cases routed to human review) are documented and communicated to the client as operating caveats, not hidden.
Sign-offs. Internal: technical lead responsible for the system signs off on testing evidence; CTO approves the release. External: client sign-off after UAT for client-operated systems.
Safeguards required at deployment:
• Phased release where feasible — limited user group or parallel run alongside the existing process before full cutover;
• Production monitoring and logging of AI outputs and agent actions, with alerting on anomalous behaviour;
• Defined rollback path allowing the AI feature to be disabled without taking down the surrounding system;
• Support and incident-response commitments (orientative SLAs of ~4h for critical and ~24h for major issues during the support period), under which AI-related incidents are triaged like any other production incident, with the added option of reverting to human-only workflow. MGOIT's AI risk management runs through the full project lifecycle on a single governance chain: (1) scoping — use-case risk assessment (EU AI Act classification, internal criteria) with authority to decline or redesign unreasonable use cases; (2) design — graduated human-in-the-loop mechanisms and, for agentic systems, scoped permissions, approval gates on write actions, and full action logging; (3) pre-deployment — testing in secure staging environments with anonymised/synthetic data, edge-case and bias checks, security and GDPR review under our ISO/IEC 27001:2022 ISMS; (4) deployment — technical lead and CTO sign-off plus client UAT acceptance, phased release and rollback paths; (5) operations — monitoring, incident management with committed SLAs, and corrective actions feeding back into risk criteria and testing checklists.
Escalation runs to the CTO and two technical leads, who hold stop/rollback authority independent of delivery deadlines. Practices are reviewed at least annually alongside the ISO 27001 ISMS review cycle, and additionally triggered by: production incidents, relevant externally reported incidents or provider advisories, significant changes in model providers or capabilities, new regulatory requirements, and the start of each new AI project (scoping acts as a de facto review). Generative AI receives additional output-quality and hallucination controls; agentic AI receives stricter autonomy-based controls as described above. AI risk management is embedded through executive and senior technical oversight, proportionate to our size (senior team of four). The CTO holds executive oversight and final accountability for AI risk decisions, including deployment approval and stop/rollback authority. The two technical leads (System Architect and Lead Software/AI Engineer) provide senior management oversight: they assess AI risks at scoping, design, and pre-deployment, sign off on testing evidence, and escalate concerns directly to the CTO. AI risk criteria are integrated into our ISO/IEC 27001:2022 ISMS, which is externally audited and reviewed at least annually, with additional updates triggered by incidents, regulatory changes, or material changes to systems or model providers.

